---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../components/SiteFooter.astro';

const title = 'Privacy Policy — 2FA COUNTER';
const description =
  '2FA COUNTER (2facounter.com) privacy policy: no accounts, no ads. Your 2FA secret keys are processed exclusively in your browser; analytics are anonymous and separate from the tool.';

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'WebPage',
    name: title,
    description,
    url: 'https://2facounter.com/privacy/',
    breadcrumb: {
      '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
      itemListElement: [
        { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: '2FA COUNTER', item: 'https://2facounter.com/' },
        { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Privacy policy', item: 'https://2facounter.com/privacy/' },
      ],
    },
  },
];
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} path="/privacy/" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <SiteHeader />
  <main id="main">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-[720px] px-4 py-20 sm:px-6 lg:py-24">
      <p class="section-label">Legal</p>
      <h1 class="mt-3 font-display text-4xl tracking-normal text-ink">Privacy policy</h1>
      <p class="mt-4 font-mono text-label font-bold text-ink">Effective · November 7, 2026 · Updated · November 10, 2026</p>

      <div class="rich-text mt-12 rounded-xl border-2 border-ink bg-cream p-6 sm:p-10" style="box-shadow: var(--shadow-card)">
        <blockquote>
          The short version: we cannot see your keys, we cannot lose them, and there is nothing
          about you to protect — because we never receive it.
        </blockquote>

        <h2>What this site collects</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Nothing that identifies you or your keys.</strong> There are no accounts, no
          form submissions, and no ad tracking pixels. The site runs two measurement tools, both
          strictly separate from the 2FA tool:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Cloudflare Web Analytics</strong> — a small, cookieless script that reports
            anonymous, aggregate performance data (page views, load times, approximate country)
            and stores nothing in your browser.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Google Analytics</strong>, loaded through a Google Tag Manager container —
            used for aggregate traffic measurement, and only after you accept the analytics
            prompt on your first visit (see <a href="#cookies">Cookies</a>). Google Analytics
            sets first-party cookies (the <code>_ga</code> family) in your browser and processes
            standard device and browser data under{" "}
            <a rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://www.google.com/policies/privacy/partners/">Google’s
            privacy terms</a>. You can opt out entirely with{" "}
            <a rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://tools.google.com/dlpage/gaoptout">Google’s
            official opt-out add-on</a>.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Neither tool receives anything you type — your keys and codes are handled entirely in
          the tool, locally. The site is statically generated HTML plus JavaScript that runs
          locally in your browser. The tool does not even write your 2FA keys to browser storage —
          they live only in the open page’s memory, as described below under{" "}
          <a href="#local-storage">Local storage</a>. Cookies are covered under{" "}
          <a href="#cookies">Cookies</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="cookies">Cookies</h2>
        <p>
          2facounter.com does not set any cookies of its own. Google Analytics, loaded through
          Google Tag Manager, sets first-party analytics cookies (the <code>_ga</code> family):
          they store a random client identifier so repeat visits can be counted as one user, and
          they contain nothing you type, paste, scan, or generate. On your first visit we ask
          before enabling these analytics cookies — decline, and Google Analytics never loads at
          all; accept, and you can change your mind later via the "Cookie settings" link in the
          footer. Cloudflare Web Analytics is
          cookieless by design and stores nothing in your browser. Blocking or deleting analytics
          cookies does not affect the generator in any way. Your keys are never profiled or
          recorded: the tool deliberately has no “remember my last key” behavior — your keys
          exist only in the open page’s memory, never in <code>localStorage</code> and never in
          cookies. More in the Local storage section below.
        </p>

        <h2 id="ads">Google advertising</h2>
        <p>
          There are no ads on the site today, and the tool works without any. This policy
          discloses in advance how advertising would work if it is ever introduced (for example,
          via Google AdSense): third-party vendors, including Google, use cookies to serve ads
          based on your prior visits to this and other websites; Google’s use of advertising
          cookies (such as the DoubleClick cookie) enables it and its partners to serve ads to
          you based on your visits to this site and/or other sites on the internet; and you may
          opt out of personalized advertising at any time by visiting{" "}
          <a rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://adssettings.google.com">Google Ads Settings</a>{" "}
          or{" "}
          <a rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://www.aboutads.info">www.aboutads.info</a>. For
          Google’s own account of how it uses data when you use our partners’ sites, see{" "}
          <a rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://www.google.com/policies/privacy/partners/">How
          Google uses data when you use our partners’ sites or apps</a>.
          Advertising cookies would never be used to read, receive, or transmit your 2FA secret
          keys — those are handled exclusively in the tool described above and are simply not in
          scope for anything beyond your own browser.
        </p>

        <h2>What never leaves your device</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Your Base32 / <code>otpauth://</code> secret keys.</li>
          <li>Screenshots and images you drop in or paste for QR scanning.</li>
          <li>Anything you type into the generator — keys are held in the page’s memory only and are never written to browser storage.</li>
          <li>Generated TOTP codes and their timers.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          If you use the camera-scan option, your browser asks permission before the tool can
          access the camera. The video stream is decoded on your device to read the QR code and is
          never recorded, stored, or transmitted; closing the scanner releases the camera.
        </p>
        <p>
          You can verify all of this yourself: open your browser’s DevTools, watch the Network
          tab, and paste a key. <strong>Zero requests carry your data.</strong> Once the page is
          loaded you can disconnect from the internet entirely — decoding QR images, generating
          codes, and copying them all keep working offline.
        </p>

        <h2 id="local-storage">Local storage</h2>
        <p>
          Nothing. The generator does not write your key, your codes, or your recent-keys list
          to <code>localStorage</code>, <code>sessionStorage</code>, cookies, or IndexedDB —
          they exist only in the open page’s memory, so refreshing or closing the page wipes
          them all. Keys previously saved under <code>2fac.key.v2</code> and{" "}
          <code>2fac.recent.v1</code> by older versions of the tool are erased from your
          browser’s storage the next time you visit.
        </p>
        <p>
          There is no cloud sync and no export file: the tool intentionally has nothing to
          back up. Keep the original secret in an encrypted, password-manager vault — that is
          your master copy, and it contains your raw secrets.
        </p>

        <h2>Third-party requests</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Two, by design.</strong> Fonts (display, text, and mono) and the
          QR-decoding library are all bundled at build time and served from this domain itself.
          The only exceptions are the two measurement tools above: Cloudflare Web Analytics — a
          small, deferred script from Cloudflare’s <code>static.cloudflareinsights.com</code> that
          reports anonymous, aggregate performance data, is cookieless, writes nothing to your
          browser, and is discarded by Cloudflare without storing IP addresses — and Google
          Tag Manager / Google Analytics, loaded from <code>www.googletagmanager.com</code>, which
          sets the first-party cookies described under <a href="#cookies">Cookies</a>. Everything
          else your browser talks to is this site only.
        </p>
        <p>
          The 2FA tool itself remains fully local: nothing you type, paste, scan, or generate
          ever leaves your device. Once the page is loaded (or the app is installed), the tool
          keeps working with the internet disconnected entirely — the analytics beacon simply
          has nothing to report while you are offline and goes quiet.
        </p>

        <h2>Standard logs</h2>
        <p>
          Like every website on the internet, our hosting provider (or CDN) may process standard
          connection metadata such as request IP addresses and user agents for security and abuse
          prevention. That processing happens outside our control and is governed by the
          provider’s own practices, however briefly the provider retains it. No 2FA data is ever
          included, because no 2FA data is ever transmitted.
        </p>

        <h2>Children</h2>
        <p>
          The tool is a general-purpose utility and does not target children under 13. It is not
          designed to collect any personal information, and it has no mechanism that sends data
          to us — there is nothing server-side to hand over or delete.
        </p>

        <h2>Changes</h2>
        <p>
          If this policy changes meaningfully, including the domain, the change will be noted
          above. Public issue? Write to <a href="/contact/">the contact page</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
  <SiteFooter />
</Layout>
